--TEST--
An observer cannot change the value a read gives back, a throwing `onPropertyGet` fails the read, a private or protected property is observed like any other, and an access to a hook's own backing slot carries no observer — `rule:classes/property-observer` and `rule:classes/property-observer-pipeline`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/propertyobserver
// `rule:classes/property-observer-pipeline`: the value is produced first and the observer is told
// afterwards, so a write this observer makes reaches the slot and never the read
// that is already in progress. Both members return `void`, which is the same
// rule stated in the signature.
final class Meddler implements PropertyObserver {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor() {
        $this->n = 7;
    }

    public function onPropertyGet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
        if ($value == 7) {
            $this->n = 99;
        }
    }

    public function onPropertySet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
    }
}

// `rule:classes/property-observer-pipeline`: "an observer that throws still fails the access,
// propagated like any other call" (`rule:errors/propagation`). The reference chapter says that of a
// write; the rule says it of both, and a read is the half nothing asked about.
final class Sealed implements PropertyObserver {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor() {
        $this->n = 1;
    }

    public function onPropertyGet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
        throw new LogicError("refusing to read " . $name);
    }

    public function onPropertySet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
    }
}

// `rule:classes/property-observer`: "every declared property of that class" is every one of them, so
// visibility exempts nothing and an access from inside the class is an access.
final class Ledger implements PropertyObserver {
    private int $count;
    protected string $tag;

    // `rule:classes/property-observer-pipeline`, third boundary: an access inside the property's own
    // hooks is the backing slot and carries no observer, or one write would be
    // reported twice. The hook's read of `$this->tag` is a different property,
    // so that one is reported.
    public string $label {
        get => $this->label;
        set (string $v) {
            $this->label = $this->tag . ":" . $v;
        }
    }

    public function constructor() {
        $this->count = 0;
        $this->tag = "row";
        $this->label = "a";
    }

    public function bump(): int {
        $this->count = $this->count + 1;
        return $this->count;
    }

    public function onPropertyGet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
        echo "  get ", $name, " = ", $value, "\n";
        Tally::$reads = Tally::$reads + 1;
    }

    public function onPropertySet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
        echo "  set ", $name, " = ", $value, "\n";
        Tally::$writes = Tally::$writes + 1;
    }
}

// `rule:classes/property-observer-pipeline`, second boundary: a `static` property has no receiving
// instance and reaches nothing, so these counters do not count themselves.
final class Tally {
    public static int $reads = 0;
    public static int $writes = 0;
}

// The read is answered by storage, and the observer's own write lands after it.
// The caller sees 7; the slot holds 99.
echo "a meddling observer:\n";
Meddler $m = new Meddler();
int $seen = $m->n;
echo "  caller sees ", $seen, "\n";
echo "  the slot now holds ", $m->n, "\n";

echo "a throwing read:\n";
Sealed $s = new Sealed();
try {
    int $never = $s->n;
    echo "  BAD, the read answered ", $never, "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "  caught ", $e->message, "\n";
}

// `private` and `protected` are written by the constructor, and `bump` reads and
// writes `$count` from inside the class.
echo "a private and a protected property:\n";
Ledger $l = new Ledger();
int $bumped = $l->bump();
echo "  bump gave ", $bumped, "\n";

// The `get` hook returns its own slot, and the observer is told once.
echo "a hooked property:\n";
string $label = $l->label;
echo "  caller sees ", $label, "\n";

// The count is the invariant: four writes and four reads reached the observer on
// this one instance, and no hook's own slot access is among them.
echo "reads=", Tally::$reads, " writes=", Tally::$writes, "\n";
--EXPECT--
a meddling observer:
  caller sees 7
  the slot now holds 99
a throwing read:
  caught refusing to read n
a private and a protected property:
  set count = 0
  set tag = row
  get tag = row
  set label = row:a
  get count = 0
  set count = 1
  get count = 1
  bump gave 1
a hooked property:
  get label = row:a
  caller sees row:a
reads=4 writes=4
